@meta
id: 08-17
title: The two-character transpose
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d, put-p
par: 32
minutes: 4
vscode: select the two letters, retype them in the other order
open: gateway.config.ts

@brief
`p` has been putting whole lines back so far, because everything you had
put in the register was a line. Take one character instead and `p` puts
one character — after the cursor, inside the line, no new line involved.

That gives you a trick worth having in your fingers, because typing
fingers make this exact mistake all day: two adjacent characters in the
wrong order. Delete the first one and put it back on the other side of
its neighbour.

Four transposed pairs in this config. `teh`, `hsot`, `prot`, `timeuot`.
Fix all four. Nothing else in the file changes.

@keys
| `x` | delete the character under the cursor, into the register |
| `p` | put it back after the cursor |
| `fe` | forward to the next `e` |
| `0` | back to column zero |

@hints
Two letters are the wrong way round. You do not need to retype either of
them — you need to move one of them one place to the right.
--
`x` takes the character and leaves the cursor on the one that was next to
it. `p` drops it back on the far side. `xp` is the whole fix.
--
On `hsot`, put the cursor on the `s`: `x` leaves `hot` with the cursor on
`o`, and `p` gives you `host`. `f` gets you onto each letter in one
press — but `j` keeps the column you were in, so press `0` after each
`j` or the `f` will start looking from halfway along the line.

@solution
    j fe xp  j0 fs xp  j0 fr xp  j0 fu xp  :wq<Enter>

`xp` is the smallest useful sentence in vim: a verb, a register and a
put, in two keystrokes. It is worth learning as a single gesture rather
than as two commands, the way `ddp` and `yyp` are.

The bigger idea is the one in the first paragraph. The register does not
hold "text"; it holds text *and a shape*. `x` put a character in it, so
`p` produced a character in the middle of a line. `dd` put a line in it,
so `p` produced a line. Same key, entirely different result, and no
setting anywhere controls it — it is decided by how the text got into the
register in the first place.

That is the single most confusing thing about paste in vim, and the next
lesson is about nothing else.

@start gateway.config.ts
export const gateway = {
  // teh gateway gives up after 30s, so keep ours under that
  hsot: 'pay.internal',
  prot: 8080,
  timeuot: 5000,
};

@expected gateway.config.ts
export const gateway = {
  // the gateway gives up after 30s, so keep ours under that
  host: 'pay.internal',
  port: 8080,
  timeout: 5000,
};
